If we sort the array, we only need to check nums[i] + nums[j] > nums[k] (with i < j < k) because the array is sorted (nums[i] <= nums[j] <= nums[k]), so the other two inequalities are automatically satisfied.

This allows us to use a two-pointer approach for efficiency.


✅ Explanation:

Sort the array for easy comparison.

Fix the largest side nums[k].

Use two pointers i and j to find all pairs (nums[i], nums[j]) such that nums[i] + nums[j] > nums[k].

If it satisfies, all elements between i and j also satisfy, so we add (j - i) to the count.

💡 Complexity:

Sorting: O(n log n)

Two-pointer check: O(n^2)

This is efficient for n <= 1000.




